<Page
    x:Class="LumigramPlus.App.ImageViewerPage"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    Background="Black">

    <!--
      The picture fits the screen first, and is zoomed up from there.

      This used to be an Image straight inside the ScrollViewer, which does not do
      that. A ScrollViewer offers its content unlimited room, so Stretch="Uniform"
      had nothing to fit into and the Image was laid out at the bitmap's own pixel
      size - several times the width of the screen for any photograph. It opened
      deep inside the top-left corner of itself, and MinZoomFactor="1" meant it
      could not be zoomed out far enough to see, only dragged around.

      The fix is to give the content a size. Stage is set to the viewport, so at
      zoom 1 the whole picture is on screen and there is nothing to scroll; zooming
      in makes the content larger than the viewport, which is the point at which
      panning starts to mean something.

      Scrollbars are hidden rather than turned off. There is nothing to scroll until
      the picture is zoomed, and once it is, two grey bars across a photograph are
      not information anybody wants.
    -->
    <ScrollViewer x:Name="Zoomer"
                  ZoomMode="Enabled" MinZoomFactor="1" MaxZoomFactor="8"
                  HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Hidden"
                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Hidden"
                  HorizontalScrollMode="Enabled" VerticalScrollMode="Enabled"
                  SizeChanged="Zoomer_SizeChanged"
                  DoubleTapped="Zoomer_DoubleTapped">
        <Grid x:Name="Stage">
            <Image x:Name="Picture" Stretch="Uniform"/>
        </Grid>
    </ScrollViewer>

    <Page.BottomAppBar>
        <CommandBar>
            <AppBarButton x:Name="SaveButton" Icon="Save" Label="save" Click="Save_Click"/>
        </CommandBar>
    </Page.BottomAppBar>
</Page>
